{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2985","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2985","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2985\",\"parent_revision\":\"34a8dd6506a7c9f11e93082aa3657354b96f1646\",\"root_revision\":\"ad8cbae99d3247d211a2d3ab44fcdf837d3d1cdadb499784618b0a7e0559f1f4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-18\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nA BacklogItem stores its description in two independent locations: the top-level `description` metadata field, and a \\\"Description\\\" entry inside `item.sections` (part of the Story/Description/Acceptance Criteria/Context template every item gets at creation). Both are populated identically at creation time via `add_item`, so they start in sync.\\n\\nCalling `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_update(selector=..., description=\\\"...\\\")` only updates the top-level field. Traced directly in `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py`: `_update_item_description()` (lines 737-748) calls `update_item_metadata(reference, {\\\"description\\\": description})` and returns \u2014 it never touches `item.sections[\\\"Description\\\"]`. The tool's response reports `\\\"description_updated\\\": true` with no indication that a second, independent copy of the same conceptual content was left unchanged.\\n\\nReproduced directly: called `backlog_update(selector=\\\"#2979\\\", description=\\\"<corrected text>\\\")`, got back `{\\\"description_updated\\\": true, \\\"changes\\\": {\\\"description\\\": true}}`. Re-read the item immediately after with `backlog_view` \u2014 the top-level `description` field showed the corrected text, but `sections.Description.entries[0].content` (and the rendered `body`'s `## Description` block \u2014 the part that becomes the actual GitHub issue body) still showed the original, uncorrected text verbatim. Only caught this by manually re-reading the item after the update; the tool's own success response gave no signal that anything was left stale.\\n\\nImpacted functionality: any caller \u2014 human or agent \u2014 using `backlog_update(description=...)` to correct or edit an item's description will silently fail to change what the item's own body/GitHub issue actually displays, while receiving a success response that implies the opposite. `backlog_view`'s compact/summary responses and any consumer reading the top-level `description` field will show corrected content; consumers reading `sections`/`body`/the rendered GitHub issue (the far more common path \u2014 grooming workflows, GitHub's own UI, `backlog view --selector` in close/start.md, etc.) will not.\\n\\nExpected outcome: `backlog_update(description=...)` should update whichever storage location(s) other tools and the rendered body actually read, or (if the two fields are genuinely intended to diverge for some reason not evident from the code read here) the tool's response should explicitly report that the Description section was NOT updated, so a caller isn't misled by a success response into believing the visible content changed.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **backlog_update's response to explain what a description update actually did** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Discovered while correcting backlog items #2979/#2984, 2026-08-18; corrected same day after checking issue comments and the actual `apply_patches()` code\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-18\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-19T00:05:36.688206Z</sub>\\n\\n**Broadened (2026-08-19):** this item's original scope (backlog_update's description-update response doesn't distinguish \\\"applied to visible body\\\" from \\\"recorded as pending human-reviewable audit comment,\\\" per the 2026-08-18 correction above) is a specific instance of a wider pattern: `backlog_core/operations.py`'s response dicts are optional-key-shaped, not typed \u2014 a key is present only when a particular code path was taken, and callers cannot distinguish \\\"absent because nothing happened\\\" from \\\"absent because a different path ran.\\\"\\n\\nA second, currently-live instance of this same pattern was found during a 2026-08-19 adversarial review of workflow-quality suggestions: `operations.py` around line 1429-1433, `add_item()`'s return dict \u2014\\n\\n```python\\n\\\"reference\\\": item_reference,\\n\\\"file_path\\\": item_reference,\\n...\\nresult[\\\"item_ref\\\"] = issue_ref   # only set when issue_ref is truthy\\n```\\n\\n`item_ref` is present only conditionally. `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_live_validation.py:232` asserts `\\\"item_ref\\\" in result` unconditionally, and this assertion is failing on `main` right now (confirmed via `gh run list --workflow \\\"Code quality\\\" --branch main`, `test-e2e` job, continuous since at least 2026-08-17). Whether this is a genuine regression or an intentionally-changed contract that the test itself is now stale against was not determined \u2014 either way, the response shape is ambiguous by construction and the ambiguity is the point.\\n\\nRecommended reframing (from the review, not a required approach): treat this as a response-contract typing problem across `backlog_core.operations`, not a single-tool response-clarity fix \u2014 consistent with this repo's own documented convention (\\\"Structured data \u2192 Pydantic, not dataclass/TypedDict\\\" in `AGENTS.md`) for MCP tool payloads.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Original (2026-08-18):** `backlog_update`'s response (`{\\\"description_updated\\\": true, \\\"changes\\\": {\\\"description\\\": true}}`) gives no indication that an audit comment was posted, that the visible body was intentionally left untouched, or that further action is needed to make the change visible. A caller reading only the response has no way to know the difference between \\\"fully applied\\\" and \\\"recorded for human review\\\" \u2014 both currently look identical in the response shape. Confirmed at the code level in `github_work_items.py`'s `apply_patches()`: a description/section update posts a GitHub Issue comment carrying a `dh-work-item-version` marker and writes a `WorkItemHead` record via the Contents API; it never edits the issue's live `body` field. This matches `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s documented \\\"GitHub writable records\\\" design (human-owned issue bodies, never auto-overwritten).\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"0e773c12f7d85a7c582c72de5fe86067b3276d5db75130de2cd007eff30c5e64\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPgviTg\"}"}